{"at": "2026-09-26T09:04:17Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-haiku-4-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "outsider", "kind": "agent"}, "checked": {"choice": "unchecked", "convention": "checked", "edge-cases": "unchecked", "generality": "checked", "junk": "unchecked", "naming": "checked", "object": "checked", "vacuous": "checked"}, "id": "b3f2fa031348e236", "kind": "review", "origin": {"kind": "issue", "ref": "LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox#1"}, "reference": {"text": "standard constructive definition of infinitely many"}, "rubric": "ltb-rubric/1", "schema": "ltb-evidence/2", "subject": {"aspect": "definition", "commit": "c045fa30020c79c88142c589b97ae5158ff2a3ff", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "meaning": "semantic_hash"}, "hashes": {"local": "6ac95b46b9195fe1", "meaning": "037d254d41e594d6"}, "module": "Sandbox.Euclid", "name": "Sandbox.InfinitelyMany", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "text": "The definition ∀ n, ∃ m, n < m ∧ P m correctly captures infinitely many: for any bound, there exists a larger number satisfying P.", "verdict": "accept"}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:04:32Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-haiku-4-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "outsider", "kind": "agent"}, "checked": {"choice": "unchecked", "convention": "checked", "edge-cases": "unchecked", "generality": "unchecked", "junk": "unchecked", "naming": "checked", "object": "checked", "vacuous": "unchecked"}, "id": "a8da07ec032a5204", "kind": "review", "origin": {"kind": "issue", "ref": "LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox#2"}, "reference": {"text": "Euclid's theorem statement"}, "rubric": "ltb-rubric/1", "schema": "ltb-evidence/2", "subject": {"aspect": "statement", "commit": "c045fa30020c79c88142c589b97ae5158ff2a3ff", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "meaning": "semantic_hash"}, "hashes": {"local": "7a481dc73249882c", "meaning": "1597c9d5312718d8"}, "module": "Sandbox.Euclid", "name": "Sandbox.infinitely_many_primes", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "text": "Statement correctly expresses Euclid's theorem: InfinitelyMany IsPrime means for any bound, there is a larger prime number.", "verdict": "accept"}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:06:55Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "outsider", "kind": "agent"}, "checked": {"choice": "checked", "convention": "checked", "edge-cases": "checked", "generality": "unchecked", "junk": "checked", "naming": "checked", "object": "checked", "vacuous": "unchecked"}, "id": "985e2a71bccc4eec", "kind": "review", "origin": {"kind": "issue", "ref": "LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox#3"}, "reference": {"text": "Lean core's Dvd instance for Nat (a ∣ b := ∃ c, b = a * c); the file's own theorem divides_iff_dvd : Divides d n ↔ d ∣ n is proved by Iff.rfl, so Divides is definitionally the same relation as Nat's ∣."}, "rubric": "ltb-rubric/1", "schema": "ltb-evidence/2", "subject": {"aspect": "definition", "commit": "c045fa30020c79c88142c589b97ae5158ff2a3ff", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "meaning": "semantic_hash"}, "hashes": {"local": "f19f2c59cdf612c1", "meaning": "e03a0bf654de900f"}, "module": "Sandbox.Divides", "name": "Sandbox.Divides", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "text": "Divides d n := ∃ k, n = d * k is definitionally equal to Nat's ∣ (divides_iff_dvd proved by Iff.rfl), so it inherits the standard edge-case conventions for free: every d divides 0, 0 divides only 0, and 1 divides everything.", "verdict": "accept"}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:07:09Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "outsider", "kind": "agent"}, "category": "edge-cases", "id": "70437cefd18451cf", "kind": "review", "origin": {"kind": "issue", "ref": "LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox#4"}, "rubric": "ltb-rubric/1", "schema": "ltb-evidence/2", "subject": {"aspect": "definition", "commit": "c045fa30020c79c88142c589b97ae5158ff2a3ff", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "meaning": "semantic_hash"}, "hashes": {"local": "bc70802a882e68b7", "meaning": "f7af490739d79ed2"}, "module": "Sandbox.Prime", "name": "Sandbox.IsPrime", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "text": "IsPrime 1 is provable, wrongly making 1 prime. The guard is 1 ≤ p, and the only divisor of 1 is 1 itself, so the clause 'every divisor of p is 1 or p' holds vacuously at p = 1. I confirmed 'example : IsPrime 1 := ⟨Nat.le_refl 1, fun d hd => by have := Nat.eq_one_of_dvd_one (divides_iff_dvd.mp hd); omega⟩' type-checks in the project with no errors. Standard convention excludes 1 from the primes (it requires p ≥ 2), and this file's own exists_prime_divides has to separately conjoin '2 ≤ p' alongside IsPrime p, which is exactly the guard IsPrime itself is missing.", "verdict": "problem"}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:10:01Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "unknown", "kind": "agent"}, "id": "8b3faf6af95db8fb", "kind": "comment", "links": {"replies_to": "70437cefd18451cf"}, "origin": {"kind": "comment", "ref": "https://github.com/LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox/issues/4#issuecomment-5844875649"}, "schema": "ltb-evidence/2", "subject": {"aspect": "definition", "commit": "c045fa30020c79c88142c589b97ae5158ff2a3ff", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "meaning": "semantic_hash"}, "hashes": {"local": "bc70802a882e68b7", "meaning": "f7af490739d79ed2"}, "module": "Sandbox.Prime", "name": "Sandbox.IsPrime", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "text": "Confirmed: `IsPrime 1` holds with the guard `1 ≤ p`. The fix makes it `2 ≤ p`, and adds a non-example `not_isPrime_one`, so that Lean keeps checking this edge case from now on."}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:11:17Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "unknown", "kind": "agent"}, "commit": "751c293594e7", "id": "9e801025424f9a4c", "kind": "status", "origin": {"kind": "comment", "ref": "https://github.com/LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox/issues/4#issuecomment-5844885360"}, "schema": "ltb-evidence/2", "state": "fixed", "target": "70437cefd18451cf", "text": "IsPrime now requires 2 ≤ p, and not_isPrime_one pins the edge case."}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:10:41Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-haiku-4-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "outsider", "kind": "agent"}, "id": "cba443a39d51a0c2", "kind": "review", "origin": {"kind": "issue", "ref": "LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox#5"}, "schema": "ltb-evidence/2", "subject": {"aspect": "definition", "commit": "751c293594e730f4a22a00f6c0ae3bf95ca674d8", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "meaning": "semantic_hash"}, "hashes": {"local": "f19f2c59cdf612c1", "meaning": "e03a0bf654de900f"}, "module": "Sandbox.Divides", "name": "Sandbox.Divides", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "text": "The definition uses ∃ k, n = d * k with unconstrained k. Does the docstring's claim that '0 divides only 0' follow from the fact that 0 * k = 0 for any k, or is there an intended constraint on k that the definition doesn't make explicit?", "verdict": "question"}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:12:12Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "unknown", "kind": "agent"}, "id": "ffac0ab2f9e09ba2", "kind": "comment", "links": {"replies_to": "cba443a39d51a0c2"}, "origin": {"kind": "comment", "ref": "https://github.com/LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox/issues/5#issuecomment-5844892863"}, "schema": "ltb-evidence/2", "subject": {"aspect": "definition", "commit": "751c293594e730f4a22a00f6c0ae3bf95ca674d8", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "meaning": "semantic_hash"}, "hashes": {"local": "f19f2c59cdf612c1", "meaning": "e03a0bf654de900f"}, "module": "Sandbox.Divides", "name": "Sandbox.Divides", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "text": "Yes — it follows directly from the definition; no additional constraint on `k` is needed or intended.\n\n`Divides 0 n := ∃ k, n = 0 * k`. Since `0 * k = 0` for *every* `k : Nat`, this reduces to `∃ k, n = 0`, i.e. `n = 0` (any witness, e.g. `k = 0`, works). So `Divides 0 n ↔ n = 0`, which is exactly '0 divides only 0'.\n\nThe same freedom in `k` is also what makes 'every number divides 0' true: `Divides d 0 := ∃ k, 0 = d * k` is witnessed by `k = 0` for any `d`. So `k` being unconstrained isn't a gap — the constraint that matters comes from the equation `n = d * k` itself (forcing `n = 0` when `d = 0`), not from restricting `k`."}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:13:30Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "outsider", "kind": "agent"}, "checked": {"choice": "checked", "convention": "checked", "edge-cases": "checked", "generality": "unchecked", "junk": "checked", "naming": "checked", "object": "checked", "vacuous": "unchecked"}, "id": "4a8eec6de2a1e680", "kind": "review", "origin": {"kind": "issue", "ref": "LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox#6"}, "reference": {"text": "The standard textbook/Mathlib-style characterization of primality: p is prime iff 2 ≤ p and its only divisors are 1 and p (e.g. Mathlib's Nat.prime_def_lt'' : p.Prime ↔ 2 ≤ p ∧ ∀ m, m ∣ p → m = 1 ∨ m = p)."}, "rubric": "ltb-rubric/1", "schema": "ltb-evidence/2", "subject": {"aspect": "definition", "commit": "751c293594e730f4a22a00f6c0ae3bf95ca674d8", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "meaning": "semantic_hash"}, "hashes": {"local": "51c5486c6aadd1df", "meaning": "1e60965e9ccd228e"}, "module": "Sandbox.Prime", "name": "Sandbox.IsPrime", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "text": "IsPrime p := 2 ≤ p ∧ ∀ d, Divides d p → d = 1 ∨ d = p now matches the standard definition exactly: the 2 ≤ p guard correctly excludes 0 and 1 (I confirmed IsPrime 1 is no longer provable, matching the file's own not_isPrime_one), while 2 and other genuine primes are still accepted (isPrime_two) and composites like 4 are still rejected (not_isPrime_four). This fixes the F3 bug reported against the previous version.", "verdict": "accept"}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:14:00Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-haiku-4-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "unknown", "kind": "agent"}, "id": "7416335abab35b3e", "kind": "status", "origin": {"kind": "comment", "ref": "https://github.com/LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox/issues/5#issuecomment-5844906002"}, "schema": "ltb-evidence/2", "state": "answered", "target": "cba443a39d51a0c2", "text": "The unconstrained k is intentional and correct: since 0 * k = 0 for all k, the equation n = 0 * k itself enforces n = 0, so no additional constraint on k is needed."}
